The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
// In mptcp_nl_remove_subflow_and_signal_addr (original):
if (entry->flags & MPTCP_PM_ADDR_FLAG_SUBFLOW) {
    spin_lock_bh(&msk->pm.lock);
    __mark_subflow_endp_available(msk, list.ids[0]);
    spin_unlock_bh(&msk->pm.lock);
}
</text>
<text>
// In mptcp_pm_remove_anno_addr (original):
if (ret) {
    __set_bit(addr->id, msk->pm.id_avail_bitmap);
    msk->pm.add_addr_signaled--;
}
</text>
<text>
// No other code sets the bitmap bit for non-subflow endpoints.
</text>
Reason:
When removing an in-kernel MPTCP endpoint that is not of type SUBFLOW (e.g., signal or initial MPC), the ID was never marked as available in `msk->pm.id_avail_bitmap`. The bit was only set for SUBFLOW endpoints (via `__mark_subflow_endp_available`) or for signal endpoints that successfully removed an announced address (via the `if (ret)` block in `mptcp_pm_remove_anno_addr`). For other cases, the ID remained marked as used even after the endpoint was removed. Later, re‑creating the endpoint with the same ID and setting the `fullmesh` flag caused `__mark_subflow_endp_available` to find the ID still marked used while `local_addr_used` was 0, leading to the kernel warning.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
// In patched mptcp_nl_remove_subflow_and_signal_addr:
spin_lock_bh(&msk->pm.lock);
if (remove_subflow)
    mptcp_pm_rm_subflow(msk, &list);
if (entry->flags & MPTCP_PM_ADDR_FLAG_SUBFLOW)
    __mark_subflow_endp_available(msk, list.ids[0]);
else /* mark endp ID as available, e.g. Signal or MPC endp */
    __set_bit(addr->id, msk->pm.id_avail_bitmap);
spin_unlock_bh(&msk->pm.lock);
</text>
<text>
// In patched mptcp_pm_remove_anno_addr:
if (ret)
    msk->pm.add_addr_signaled--;
// (the __set_bit is removed from here)
</text>
<text>
// No other changes; the bitmap is now always set for non-subflow types.
</text>
Reason:
The fix moves the responsibility of setting the ID‑available bit to the main removal function `mptcp_nl_remove_subflow_and_signal_addr` for all endpoint types. For SUBFLOW endpoints, the existing helper `__mark_subflow_endp_available` is still used. For all other types (signal, initial MPC, etc.), `__set_bit(addr->id, msk->pm.id_avail_bitmap)` is now called unconditionally after acquiring the lock, ensuring the ID is marked available whenever the endpoint is removed. The old conditional `__set_bit` inside `mptcp_pm_remove_anno_addr` is removed because it is now redundant—the bit is already set in the caller. This guarantees that no ID remains incorrectly marked as used after endpoint removal, preventing the inconsistent state that triggered the warning.

